# Optics 32 — Gaussian beams: the complex q parameter, a lens, and the paraxial wave equation
#
# (Siegman, Lasers, ch. 17 and §20.1; Saleh & Teich §3.1–3.2; Yariv, Optical
#  Electronics §2.5–2.6; Self, Appl. Opt. 22, 658 (1983).)
# A Nd:YAG beam (λ = 1064 nm) has its waist w₀ = 0.500 mm at z = 0. In free space
# q(z) = z + i z_R with z_R = π w₀²/λ, and 1/q = 1/R(z) − i λ/(π w(z)²).
#   (a) z_R, and w and R at the lens position z = 1.00 m, read off from 1/q.
#   (b) A thin lens f = 100 mm at z = 1.00 m: 1/q' = 1/q − 1/f. The new waist is where
#       q' + s is purely imaginary, s = −Re q', with w₀' = √(λ Im(q')/π). Compare with
#       Self's lens formula  s/f = 1 + (z/f − 1)/((z/f − 1)² + (z_R/f)²),
#       w₀' = w₀ / √((1 − z/f)² + (z_R/f)²).
#   (c) The Gouy phase gained from the old waist to the lens, atan(z/z_R).
#   (d) Check free-space spreading with the paraxial wave equation itself. For a beam
#       that is Gaussian in one transverse direction, u(x, z) obeys
#         2 i k ∂u/∂z = −∂²u/∂x²,  k = 2π/λ,
#       a Schrödinger equation with z as time. Start with u = (2/(π w₀²))^(1/4) e^(−x²/w₀²),
#       solve to z = 1.00 m, and compare the rms width with w(z)/2.

λ = 1064 nm
w0 = 0.500 mm
z_R = π w0² / λ
z1 = 1.00 m
q1 = z1 + 1i z_R
invq = 1 / q1
w1 = √(-λ / (π im(invq)))
R1 = 1 / re(invq)
print "(a) z_R =", z_R to 10 digits, " w(1 m) =", w1 in mm to 10 digits, " R(1 m) =", R1 to 10 digits
print "    closed forms: w0 sqrt(1 + (z/z_R)²) =", w0 √(1 + (z1 / z_R)²) in mm to 10 digits, " z (1 + (z_R/z)²) =", z1 (1 + (z_R / z1)²) to 10 digits

f = 100 mm
q2 = 1 / (1 / q1 - 1 / f)
s = -re(q2)
w0new = √(λ im(q2) / π)
print "(b) new waist at s =", s in mm to 10 digits, " w0' =", w0new in μm to 10 digits
Z = z1 / f - 1
print "    Self: s =", f (1 + Z / (Z² + (z_R / f)²)) in mm to 10 digits, " w0' =", w0 / √(Z² + (z_R / f)²) in μm to 10 digits

print "(c) Gouy phase to the lens =", atan(z1 / z_R) in ° to 8 digits

# (d) the paraxial wave equation, as the time-dependent Schrödinger equation of §20
k = 2π / λ
solve 2 i k ∂u/∂z = -∂²u/∂x²
    with u(x, 0 m) = (2 / (π w0²))^(1/4) exp(-x² / w0²), u(-6 mm, z) = 0 m^(-1/2), u(6 mm, z) = 0 m^(-1/2)
    for x from -6 mm to 6 mm, z from 0 m to 1.00 m
    grid 2000
norm = ∫ |u(x, z1)|^2 dx from -6 mm to 6 mm
rms = √((∫ x² |u(x, z1)|^2 dx from -6 mm to 6 mm) / norm)
print "(d) norm =", norm to 8 digits, " rms width =", rms in mm to 6 digits, " w(z)/2 =", w1 / 2 in mm to 6 digits
